Don't clobber a dynamic SPARC npc when tracing an instruction.

With a UC_HOOK_CODE hook active (which includes the hook emu_start installs
for a `count` limit), the SPARC translator syncs pc and npc into the CPU state
before each traced instruction.  It wrote dc->npc unconditionally, but after a
jmpl or rett dc->npc is the DYNAMIC_PC marker (1), not an address, and cpu_npc
already holds the computed jump target.  Storing the marker over it sent every
indirect jump -- ret, retl, jmp, call through a register -- to address 1 after
its delay slot, which raised TT_UNALIGNED (interrupt 7).

Sync npc only when it is a static address.  A conditional branch's JUMP_PC
npc is resolved from the branch condition at the end of the block, so leaving
cpu_npc alone there changes nothing.

--- a/qemu/target/sparc/translate.c	2026-10-21 11:16:13.956824981 -0400
+++ b/qemu/target/sparc/translate.c	2026-10-21 11:16:14.065825321 -0400
@@ -5964,7 +5964,13 @@
 
         // Sync PC in advance
         tcg_gen_movi_tl(tcg_ctx, tcg_ctx->cpu_pc, dc->pc);
-        tcg_gen_movi_tl(tcg_ctx, tcg_ctx->cpu_npc, dc->npc);
+        // Only a static npc can be synced this way.  DYNAMIC_PC and JUMP_PC
+        // are translator markers, not addresses: after a jmpl/rett, cpu_npc
+        // already holds the computed target, and storing the marker over it
+        // sends the delay slot's successor to address 1.
+        if (dc->npc != DYNAMIC_PC && dc->npc != JUMP_PC) {
+            tcg_gen_movi_tl(tcg_ctx, tcg_ctx->cpu_npc, dc->npc);
+        }
 
         gen_uc_tracecode(tcg_ctx, 4, UC_HOOK_CODE_IDX, uc, dc->pc);
         // the callback might want to stop emulation immediately
